@@ -0,0 +1,39 @@
+---
+tier: quick
+created: 2026-09-20
+status: in-progress
+owner: node
+branch: HEAD
+touches: [src/flask/app.py, tests/test_appctx.py, tests/test_reqctx.py]
+---
+
+# teardown-resilience
+
+## Why
+During request or application-context teardown, one failing callback currently
+prevents later callbacks and the corresponding teardown signal from running.
+
+## What
+- Run every registered teardown callback and the relevant teardown signal.
+- Collect callback and signal failures, then raise them after cleanup.
+- Restore context-local state before the collected failure leaves context pop.
+- Do not change unrelated request processing or signal semantics.
+
+## Acceptance
+- [x] Request teardown attempts all callbacks and its signal after earlier
+  failures, then reports their errors. — check: `tests/test_appctx.py`
+- [x] Application-context teardown has the same behavior and restores the
+  context before reporting errors. — check: `tests/test_appctx.py`
+- [x] Python 3.11+ preserves all errors in an exception group; older Python
+  raises the first collected error. — check: code review and `tests/test_appctx.py`
+
+## Impact
+`AppContext.pop` is the sole request and application-context teardown entry
+point. It invokes the two public `Flask.do_teardown_*` methods, closes the
+request, restores `_cv_app`, and emits `appcontext_popped`. The new collection
+path must preserve callback order and the existing teardown signals while
+letting the reset and later application teardown run after request failures.
+
+## Decisions
+- D-17: collect teardown callback and signal failures, then raise them after
+  cleanup; on Python 3.10, raise the first because exception groups are absent.
